.TITLE "say"

.DIV "DESCRIPTION"
Converts text to spoken audio using the built-in macOS text-to-speech engine.
This command allows users to make their Mac speak any provided text, either from
standard input, a file, or directly from the command-line arguments. It supports
various voices and speaking rates.

.DIV "ALIAS"
**speak**

.DIV "TLDR"
**say "__Hello World__"**
Speaks the phrase "__Hello World__" using the default system voice.
> __"Hello World"__: The text string to be spoken.

**say -v __"Samantha"__ "__Good morning__"**
Speaks the phrase "__Good morning__" using the specified voice, e.g., "__Samantha__".
> __"Samantha"__: The name of the voice to use. | __"Good morning"__: The text to be spoken.

**say -f __file.txt__**
Reads and speaks the content of a specified __file.txt__.
> __file.txt__: The path to the text file to be spoken.

**say -o __output.aiff__ "__Hello__"**
Saves the spoken audio of "__Hello__" to an __output.aiff__ file instead of speaking aloud.
> __output.aiff__: The path to the output audio file. | __"Hello"__: The text to be spoken.

.DIV "SUMMARY"
The `say` command is useful for accessibility, creating audio notifications,
or simply having your Mac read out documents. You can customize voices and
their properties in System Settings under Accessibility > Spoken Content.

.DIV "SEE ALSO"
**mac:afplay**

documentation: https://ss64.com/osx/say.html
